      "type": "text",
      "title": "Pinhole Clients",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Pinhole is accessed via two clients: a mobile app (iOS and Android) and a web app."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P1.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n16",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Client API Interaction",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Both clients use the same public HTTPS API and only perform basic input checks, without implementing business rules."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P1.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n17",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Direct Photo Uploads",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Clients upload photos directly to object storage using a short-lived signed URL, preventing large files from passing through Pinhole's services."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P1.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n18",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "API Gateway Entry Point",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "All client requests first go through the API gateway."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P2.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n19",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Gateway Functions",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The API gateway handles TLS termination, validates access tokens with the auth service, enforces a rate limit of 120 requests per minute per account, and routes requests to the appropriate service."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P2.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n20",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Auth Service Unreachable",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "If the auth service is unavailable, the gateway rejects the request instead of allowing it to proceed unchecked."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P2.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n21",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Photo Serving",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Photo bytes are delivered to clients via the CDN, not the API gateway."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P2.4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n22",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Auth Service Responsibilities",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The auth service manages account and session data, and is responsible for issuing and verifying access tokens."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P3.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n23",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Upload Service Workflow",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The upload service provides clients with a signed upload URL. Once the upload completes, it records the new photo in the metadata database with a 'processing' status and publishes a PhotoUploaded event to the message queue."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P3.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n24",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Feed Service Functionality",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The feed service constructs user feeds by reading photo metadata from the metadata database for followed accounts and stores ready-made feed pages in the cache."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P3.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n25",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Media Processing Subscription",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Media processing workers subscribe to PhotoUploaded events from the message queue."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P4.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n26",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Thumbnail Generation",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "For each PhotoUploaded event, a worker retrieves the original photo from object storage, generates thumbnails in three sizes (150, 640, 1080 pixels wide), saves them back to object storage, and updates the photo's status to 'ready' in the metadata database."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P4.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n27",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "PhotoReady Event",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "After processing, the worker publishes a PhotoReady event."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P4.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n28",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Feed Update and Notifications",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The feed service subscribes to PhotoReady events to add the newly processed photo to followers' feeds and requests the push-notification provider to alert followers with notifications enabled."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P4.4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n29",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Message Queue Retention",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The message queue retains events for up to 7 days, ensuring that worker outages only delay thumbnail generation and do not result in lost uploads."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P5.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n30",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Failed Photo Processing",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Photos that repeatedly fail processing remain in the 'processing' state and are not displayed in any feed."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P5.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n31",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Object Storage Contents",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Object storage stores original photos and their thumbnails. Original photos are kept unchanged and are never overwritten."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P6.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n32",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Metadata Database as Source of Truth",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The metadata database stores follow relationships and photo records, including their processing status. It is the definitive source for photo existence."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P6.2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n33",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Cache Contents and Lifespan",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The cache stores ready-made feed pages for 60 seconds."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P6.3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "n34",
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Cache as a Copy",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The cache is a copy for performance; its loss only slows down feeds, it does not cause incorrect data."
      },
      "cites": [
        "P6.4"
      ]
    },
    {
