      "type": "text",
      "origin": "analysis",
      "title": "System Overview",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The Pinhole system architecture is designed for scalability and resilience, separating client interactions, edge services, core business logic, and asynchronous media processing. Direct uploads to object storage and CDN delivery for photos offload significant traffic from core services."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "analysis",
      "title": "Key Data Flows",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Photo uploads flow from clients directly to object storage, with metadata managed by the upload service. Asynchronous processing ensures that thumbnail generation and feed updates happen without blocking user interactions. The message queue plays a crucial role in decoupling these processes."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "analysis",
      "title": "Resilience and Consistency",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The system prioritizes data integrity: the metadata database is the source of truth, and the message queue's retention policy prevents upload loss during worker outages. The cache is used for speed, not correctness, ensuring data consistency even if it's lost."
      }
